WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.010
A lunch has one sandwich, cheese or bean, and one fruit,

00:00:04.220 --> 00:00:05.350
apple, pear or plum.

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:07.610
Cheese may not be paired with plum.

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:09.630
How many different lunches are possible?

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:15.770
The restriction only mentions cheese,

00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:17.290
so split by sandwich.

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:20.800
A lunch has exactly one sandwich, so these two

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:22.130
cases cannot overlap.

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:26.330
With cheese, plum is not allowed, so the allowed fruits

00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:27.600
are apple and pear.

00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:30.390
That is two lunches: cheese with apple,

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:31.860
and cheese with pear.

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:35.030
With bean, all three fruits are allowed.

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:36.590
That is three lunches.

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:39.370
Add the cases: two plus three is five.

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:41.320
Check another way.

00:00:41.810 --> 00:00:43.350
Without the rule there would be two times

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:44.620
three, six lunches.

00:00:45.110 --> 00:00:47.980
Exactly one of them, cheese with plum, is forbidden.

00:00:48.490 --> 00:00:49.900
Six minus one is five.

00:00:50.690 --> 00:00:51.600
Two methods agree.

00:00:52.610 --> 00:00:54.540
When branches have different sizes,

00:00:54.990 --> 00:00:56.320
add them rather than multiplying.

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:59.660
Rebuild the tree yourself, then try this:

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:02.440
there are three shirts and four pairs of shorts,

00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:04.910
but the red shirt cannot be worn with the

00:01:04.910 --> 00:01:05.960
two orange pairs.

00:01:06.770 --> 00:01:08.140
How many outfits are possible?
